=== r5_legacy_suppression.py
REPRODUCED R5-01 after the blessed 1.1->1.2.1 upgrade a 1.1 protection-order suppression is void for every keyed check: a detective hot-lists the protected plate, sees its hit with plate+camera+time in /hits, verifies it and exports it; the supervisor queue is empty and the order cannot be released (404). Only the plate-text SQL filters (/search here; share and Tier 3 use the same filter) still hide the plate. :: {"legacy_row": [["pref:0457f6b192bacedcbcabe5c9", "PRT100", "AR", 1]], "legacy_ref_equals_canonical_ref": false, "hotlist_add": 201, "read": {"result": "ALERT", "hit_id": 1, "hit_ids": [1], "read_id": 1}, "hits_visible_to_detective": [{"id": 1, "ts": "2026-11-24T16:19:14Z", "cam": "CAM-02", "read_plate": "PRT100", "entry_plate": "PRT100", "state": "AR", "list_name": "Local agency list", "category": "warrant", "status": "PENDING", "suppressed": 0}], "detective_verify": 200, "detective_export": 201, "supervisor_queue_has_hit": false, "release": [404, {"error": "no active suppression for that plate"}], "search_reads": []}
=== r5_hotlist_expiry.py
REPRODUCED R5-02 a hot-list entry whose expiry is typed as a US date (12/31/2027) is accepted 201 and then treated as already expired (so is a Unix epoch value): the stolen car's first read stores silently, the entry deactivates, no ALERT; hotlist_sync kills such entries nightly. A PAST date in words ('Dec 31 2025') never expires: it still alerts and survives the sync. :: {"add_us_date": [201, {"result": "added"}], "read_us_date": {"result": "stored", "read_id": 2}, "read_iso_date": {"result": "ALERT", "hit_id": 1, "hit_ids": [1], "read_id": 3}, "add_past_date_in_words": 201, "read_past_date_in_words": {"result": "ALERT", "hit_id": 2, "hit_ids": [2], "read_id": 4}, "past_date_in_words_after_sync": [["STL503", 1]], "add_epoch": 201, "read_epoch": {"result": "stored", "read_id": 1}, "rows": [["STL501", "12/31/2027", 0], ["STL502", "2027-12-31", 1], ["STL503", "Dec 31 2025", 1], ["STL505", "1798761600", 0]], "HOTLIST_EXPIRED": [{"by": "ingest", "plate_ref": "pref:142142944ef5b5b07ed8381f"}, {"by": "ingest", "plate_ref": "pref:98e775486d27ca65a89979e2"}], "sync": "hotlist_sync: swept 1 expired memberships; 2 active memberships remain", "STL504_after_sync": [["STL504", 0]]}
=== r5_supersede.py
REPRODUCED R5-03a Frigate's update stream A(0.80) -> B(0.85) -> end A(0.90), confidence rising throughout, ends with Janus holding the misread B and the stolen-plate ALERT for A deleted (the bridge drops end-A as a duplicate of update-A) :: {"update_A": {"result": "ALERT", "hit_id": 1, "hit_ids": [1], "read_id": 1}, "update_B": {"result": "superseded", "read_id": 1}, "end_A": {"forwarded": false, "reason": "duplicate event"}, "stored": [["A8C1234", 0.85]], "pending_hits_for_A": []}
REPRODUCED R5-03b a delayed partial OCR (0.61) arriving after the final read (0.93) overwrites it and deletes the stolen-plate ALERT — 'different plate' supersedes regardless of confidence or event order :: {"end": {"result": "ALERT", "hit_id": 2, "hit_ids": [2], "read_id": 2}, "late_update": {"result": "superseded", "read_id": 2}, "stored": [["STL77", 0.61]], "pending_hits_for_final": []}
REPRODUCED R5-03c a same-plate redelivery at higher confidence deletes the PENDING hit (and its outcome record) and re-creates it under a new id: the officer verifying the hit id they were alerted with gets 409 :: {"first": {"result": "ALERT", "hit_id": 3, "hit_ids": [3], "read_id": 3}, "redelivery": {"result": "ALERT", "hit_id": 4, "hit_ids": [4], "read_id": 3, "superseded": true}, "verify_original_id": [409, {"error": "hit not found or already decided"}], "outcome_rows_for_original": 0}
=== r5_evidence_deadend.py
REPRODUCED R5-04 an exported, unreleased evidence bundle outlives its hit's 150-day purge and then can never be released (404), dispositioned (404) or destroyed (409); destroy_by is never set, retention never destroys it, and /health and sentinel_audit both report the evidence store clean :: {"retention": "retention: purged by registry: hits.read_plate=1", "hit_rows_left": 0, "evidence_row": ["hit-1-20261124T161915Z", null, null, null], "bundle_on_disk": true, "release": [404, {"error": "no such hit"}], "disposition": [404, {"error": "no such hit"}], "destroy": [409, {"error": "destruction requires a release (prosecutor sign-off or a confirmed disposition) first"}], "health_retention_breach": [], "sentinel_audit_evidence": [{"detail": "within its class (evidence) per the shared registry check", "name": "registry: evidence bundles", "status": "ok"}]}
=== r5_parity.py
REPRODUCED R5-05a with retention just run (twice), an unprotected 30.5-day-old hit and a cutoff-day case_searches row are left by the purge but named as breaches by /health, alert_check and sentinel_audit --db — the 'one implementation' disagrees with the purger, so a healthy busy deployment is permanently red :: {"retention_runs": ["retention: purged by registry: nothing", "retention: purged by registry: nothing"], "hits_left": [1, 2], "case_searches_left": [["2026-11-10"]], "health_ok": false, "retention_breach": ["case_searches.frag", "hits.entry_plate", "hits.read_plate"], "alert_check": "NOCTUA ALERT: janus degraded\n  - RETENTION BREACH \u2014 registered stores past their class: case_searches.frag, hits.entry_plate, hits.read_plate\n  - cameras silent: CAM-01, CAM-02, CAM-03, CAM-04, CAM-05, CAM-08, CAM-09, CAM-101", "sentinel_audit_fails": ["registry: hits.read_plate", "registry: hits.entry_plate", "registry: case_searches.frag"]}
REPRODUCED R5-05b a held hit 150.5 days old survives two retention runs: the statutory 150-day cap is enforced as 'more than 150 WHOLE days' (up to ~2 days late with a daily run) :: {"hit_ages_days": {"1": 30.5, "2": 150.5}, "hits_left": [1, 2]}
=== r5_gate_order.py
REPRODUCED R5-06 after a FAILING audit closes the share gate, an OLDER passing report — the identical signed body re-posted, or a 2h-old pass delivered late — is accepted 201 and reopens the gate: 'latest' is filing order, not audit time, and duplicate report hashes are accepted :: {"share_open": 200, "pass_filed": 201, "fail_filed": 201, "share_after_fail": [403, {"error": "SHARE_BLOCKED", "missing": "cross_audit_ok", "agency": "agency-a"}], "same_pass_refiled": [201, {"result": "audit result recorded", "agency": "agency-a", "report_hash": "4c64e14d5417030440e070de8c679e59e31ec70e03a3cbe55a7dfd5e21fbb848"}], "share_after_refile": 200, "fail_refiled": 201, "share_closed_again": 403, "late_2h_pass_filed": 201, "share_after_late_pass": 200, "duplicate_report_hashes": [["4c64e14d5417030440e070de8c679e59e31ec70e03a3cbe55a7dfd5e21fbb848", 2], ["fce8c8485bab5fd02001413d1be54e5d138552b79aa614453e7b0543e9ade542", 2]], "partner_list": [{"agency": "agency-a", "last_audit_ok": true, "last_audit_ts": "2026-11-24T14:19:17Z", "share_gate_open": true}]}
=== r5_rotation_grind.py
REPRODUCED R5-07 the publishing supervisor still chooses who audits whom: grinding cycle_start offline (chain untouched) or nudging the chain root with an ordinary chained GET yields the wanted pair in a few tries, and Janus publishes it 201 with a valid signature :: {"wanted": ["agency-a", "agency-b"], "cycle_start_tries": 1, "published_with_cycle_start": "2027-01-01", "publish": 201, "pair_in_schedule": true, "root_nudges_needed": 3, "publish2": 201, "pair_in_schedule2": true, "offline_prediction_equals_published": [true, true], "mean_tries_over_300_trials": 3.03}
=== r5_gates.py
REPRODUCED R5-08 the outside auditor's mandatory post-hoc review of an EMERGENCY Tier 3 search is pre-empted: a second supervisor marks it justified and the auditor is then refused (409); the auditor's own views show only hashes :: {"auditor_view": [{"request_id": 1, "requester": "sgt.reyes", "case_hash": "cref:6be6abf57acca7a73357485a", "created_ts": "2026-11-24T16:19:18Z", "emergency": true, "plate_ref": "pref:ec46c2f427781be520b33392", "basis_type": "other"}, {"id": 1, "request_id": 1, "requester": "sgt.reyes", "created_ts": "2026-11-24T16:19:18Z", "emergency": true, "needs_review": true, "basis_type": "other", "tier": 3, "case_hash": "cref:6be6abf57acca7a73357485a", "plate_ref": "pref:ec46c2f427781be520b33392"}], "supervisor_review": 200, "auditor_review": [409, {"error": "that use is not awaiting review"}]}
REPRODUCED R5-09 a detective places a hold with NO case on a hit already REJECTED as a misread; retention keeps the innocent driver's plate/camera/time past HOLD_DAYS (control hit deleted); one supervisor then releases the hold with no disposition :: {"hold": [201, {"result": "hold placed", "hold_id": 1, "review_by": "2026-12-24"}], "hits_left_after_retention": [1], "held": 1, "control": 2, "release": 200}
REPRODUCED R5-11 a Tier 3 historical search executes on basis_type='other' with free-text auth_basis ('verbal OK from the lieutenant'): no judicial instrument, no number, no uploaded authorization :: {"request": [201, {"result": "PENDING \u2014 nothing was searched", "request_id": 2}], "approve": [200, {"result": "EXECUTED", "request_id": 2, "results_count": 0, "results_url": "/tier3/results/2"}]}
REPRODUCED R5-10 a case list whose feed stopped 30 days ago never goes stale: searches run against it and /health reports the config fresh (staleness = failed reload only) :: {"search": 200, "config": {"users": {"stale": false, "stale_since": null}, "cases": {"stale": false, "stale_since": null}, "cameras": {"stale": false, "stale_since": null}, "fail_closed": false}}
=== r5_minor.py
REPRODUCED R5-12 INGEST_REJECTED writes the caller's raw cam text into the permanent chain (plate-shaped or free text, readable by partners' chain_auditors) with no throttle :: {"raw_cams_in_chain": ["Doe residence driveway, 12 Elm St", "ABC1234"], "entries_added": 42, "rejected_entries": 42}
REPRODUCED R5-15 a Tier 2 search typed with a hyphen ('HYP-123') misses the stored canonical plate 'HYP123' — fragments are not normalized like plates :: {"HYP-123": [], "HYP123": [{"id": 1, "ts": "2026-11-24T16:19:18Z", "cam": "CAM-01", "plate": "HYP123", "state": "AR"}]}
REPRODUCED R5-16 a Tier 3 request pending past RETENTION_DAYS reaches the approver with why/auth_basis = null and the fragment scrubbed to an HMAC, and approval still EXECUTES (a search for the HMAC string, chained as a Tier 3 search) :: {"approver_sees": [{"id": 1, "request_id": 1, "requester": "det.okafor", "created_ts": "2026-11-04T16:19:18Z", "emergency": false, "needs_review": false, "basis_type": "warrant", "tier": 3, "plate_fragment": "pref:9482690bb3864f8bd69d686d", "case_no": "2027-1014", "why": null, "auth_basis": null, "case_hash": "cref:d25203c6ba26162249cc65d3", "plate_ref": "pref:56ec27c85d877a049179b089"}], "approve": [200, {"result": "EXECUTED", "request_id": 1, "results_count": 0, "results_url": "/tier3/results/1"}]}
REPRODUCED R5-13 over an unpinned 1.1 chain, a WRONG key placed in the key file is pinned and Janus serves (the A28 refusal covers env keys only) — although the chain's own QUERY plate_ref recomputes under the true key and not under this one :: {"served_after_wrong_key": 200, "pinned_fp_is_wrong_key": true, "chained_ref_matches_true_key": true}
REPRODUCED R5-14 a successful restore deletes the pre-restore copies of the live audit key and signing key it just moved aside (os.replace keeps their old mtime; the prune that follows is by mtime) — only the DB, -wal and -shm copies survive :: {"restore_exit": 0, "pruned_lines": ["pruned pre-restore copy janus.db.signing_pub.pem.pre-restore-20261124T161919Z (> 14 days)", "pruned pre-restore copy janus.db.audit_key.pre-restore-20261124T161919Z (> 14 days)", "pruned pre-restore copy janus.db.signing_key.pem.pre-restore-20261124T161919Z (> 14 days)"], "pre_restore_copies_left": ["janus.db-shm.pre-restore-20261124T161919Z", "janus.db-wal.pre-restore-20261124T161919Z", "janus.db.pre-restore-20261124T161919Z"]}
=== r5_restore_envkey.py
REPRODUCED R5-17 on an env-key deployment restored from a shell without NOCTUA_AUDIT_KEY, a set HMAC'd under a key the attacker shipped INSIDE the set (no Ed25519 signature) is accepted although the host's signing_pub.pem is present; Janus then boots normally with the real env key and the attacker's supervisor account works :: {"host_has_signing_pub": true, "restore_exit": 0, "restore_out": "lter\nconfig restored into /tmp/noctua12-tests-v3x1pfp9/r5-envkey/config-restored: users.json, cases.json, cameras.json\nrestored OK: chain verifies, 4 entries, root ab45bbaaf32e62d2\u2026\nprevious live files kept as *.pre-restore-20261124T161920Z", "evil_user_installed": true, "janus_up_after": true, "evil_sup_GET_hits": 200}
